<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Solving a trigonometric equation for 0° ≤ x ≤ 360° (sine)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Solve the equation</p>
\[ {@eqn@} \qquad \text{for } 0^\circ \le x \le 360^\circ. \]


<p><strong>(a)</strong> Rearrange the equation to find the exact value of \(\sin x\). </p>
<div>\(\sin x = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prta]]</div>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> Find the reference angle \(\alpha\), in degrees. </p>
<div>\(\alpha = \) [[input:ans2]]\(^\circ\) [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prtb]]</div>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> Hence find <strong>all</strong> the solutions of the equation for \(0^\circ \le x \le 360^\circ\). Type them as a list, for example <code>[40, 140]</code>. </p>
<div>\(x = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prtc]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p><strong>(a) Rearranging the equation</strong></p>
<p>Get \(\sin x\) on its own, exactly as you would solve \({@m@}y + \left({@d@}\right) = 0\) for \(y\).</p>
<p><strong>Step 1: move the constant to the other side.</strong></p>
\[ {@m@}\sin x = {@-d@}. \]
<p><strong>Step 2: divide both sides by \({@m@}\).</strong></p>
\[ \sin x = \frac{ {@-d@} }{ {@m@} } = {@k@}. \]

<p><strong>(b) The reference angle</strong></p>
<p>The reference angle \(\alpha\) is the acute angle whose sine is the <em>size</em> of \(\sin x\), ignoring the sign. From the special triangles, \(\sin {@alpha@}^\circ = {@abs(k)@}\), so</p>
\[ \alpha = {@alpha@}^\circ. \]

<p><strong>(c) Finding all the solutions</strong></p>
<p><strong>Step 1: decide which quadrants.</strong> \(\sin x = {@k@}\) is [[if test="is(sg=1)"]]<em>positive</em>. By the CAST rule, sine is positive in <strong>Quadrants I and II</strong>[[else]]<em>negative</em>. By the CAST rule, sine is positive only in Quadrants I and II, so it is negative in <strong>Quadrants III and IV</strong>[[/if]]. There is one solution in each of these quadrants.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: find the angle in each quadrant from \(\alpha = {@alpha@}^\circ\).</strong></p>
<ul>
[[if test="is(sg=1)"]]<li>Quadrant I: \(x = \alpha = {@alpha@}^\circ\).</li>
<li>Quadrant II: \(x = 180^\circ - \alpha = 180^\circ - {@alpha@}^\circ = {@180-alpha@}^\circ\).</li>[[else]]<li>Quadrant III: \(x = 180^\circ + \alpha = 180^\circ + {@alpha@}^\circ = {@180+alpha@}^\circ\).</li>
<li>Quadrant IV: \(x = 360^\circ - \alpha = 360^\circ - {@alpha@}^\circ = {@360-alpha@}^\circ\).</li>[[/if]]
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 3: check the range and write the answer.</strong> Both angles lie between \(0^\circ\) and \(360^\circ\), so</p>
\[ x = {@x1@}^\circ \quad\text{or}\quad x = {@x2@}^\circ. \]
<p>On the graph of \(y = \sin x\), the horizontal line \(y = {@k@}\) crosses the curve exactly twice between \(0^\circ\) and \(360^\circ\), at these two angles.</p>
<div style="text-align:center;" role="img" aria-label="Graph of y = sin x for x from 0 to 360 degrees, with the horizontal line y = {#float(k)#} crossing it at the two solutions.">
[[jsxgraph width="460px" height="300px"]]
